{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfdwWdFBXck6mPqWNNDXx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote the candidate `designed_exemplar_similarity`, and it passes the admission self-check (`OK`). Novelty against the other models is still unchecked, and the smoke fit raised two warnings that matter for the full admission fit (below).\n\n**The hypothesis:** people spot a \"designed\" sequence by how similar it is to a few remembered designed examples, not by checking separate structural features. The examples are a solid streak, strict alternation, any short chunk repeated end to end (at every phase), and two solid halves. Similarity is summed over those examples and falls off exponentially with the number of flips that differ; how fast it falls off is a parameter fitted from the data. Looking close to a designed example makes a sequence look less random. Each person has their own gain on this resemblance, which aims at the critique's main finding (people differ more in how often they agree with the majority than the current best model produces).\n\n**Why it isn't purely exemplar-based:** the three earlier exemplar-only models lost by 290–1090 nats. So this one keeps the best model's basic sense of a random coin: a personal ideal switching rate (lenient toward too much alternation), personal weights on heads/tails imbalance and the longest streak, and a personal guessing rate. The exemplar similarity replaces the best model's five hand-built structure cues (final streak, mirror symmetry, complement symmetry, run-length variety, most lopsided stretch). Its distinctive prediction is that a near-miss of a designed pattern, such as one flip off strict alternation, gets rejected even though it has none of those exact structures.\n\n**Smoke-fit results** (one chain, 100 tune + 100 draws):\n- ELPD-LOO was −3590.7. That number is rough and not directly comparable to the best model's −3451.1 from a full fit.\n